digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N10" [label="(10,0,0) OmpR/PhoB family response regulators (Rs) with winged-helix DNA binding motifs function as dual functional transcription regulators."];
  "N102" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: PhoP regulates complex promoters with multiple PHO boxes, but molecular details remain unclear. Abduction-knowledge: Structural determination can reveal molecular mechanisms of protein-DNA interactions. Abduction: Determining structures of PhoP-promoter complexes can reveal how PhoP controls complex promoters."];
  "N104" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-case: PhoP binds PHO boxes as dimers with specific structural arrangements. Induction-common: Transcription activators recruit RNAP through protein-protein and protein-DNA interactions. Induction: PhoP likely activates transcription through specific binding arrangements and RNAP interactions."];
  "N107" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: PhoP regulates promoters with varying numbers of PHO boxes (2, 4, or 6 boxes). Abduction-knowledge: Different oligomeric states of regulators can recognize different promoter architectures. Abduction: PhoP may form different oligomeric states (dimer, tetramer, hexamer) to recognize promoters with different numbers of PHO boxes."];
  "N14" [label="(14,0,0) Mtb PhoP regulates over 100 genes involved in virulence, metabolism, and stress response, functioning as both activator and repressor."];
  "N15" [label="(17,0,0) PhoP represses nitrogen metabolism gene amtB through cross-talk with GlnR."];
  "N17" [label="(25,0,0) PhoP regulates multiple PHO promoters consisting of two or more PHO boxes, similar to homolog PhoB."];
  "N18" [label="(26,0,0) Molecular details of how PhoP-like OmpR/PhoB family regulators efficiently control complex promoters remain to be elucidated."];
  "N19" [label="(20,0,0) Mtb PhoP comprises conserved N-terminal receiver domain (REC) and C-terminal DNA binding domain (DBD)."];
  "N20" [label="(21,0,0) Phosphorylation of REC by PhoR or AcP enhances DNA binding affinity of DBD."];
  "N21" [label="(22,0,0) PhoP binds to PHO boxes, each carrying two conserved binding sites (site a and site b) separated by several nucleotides."];
  "N22" [label="(23,0,0) PhoP dimer interacts with PHO box through conserved winged helices, similar to other OmpR/PhoB family regulators."];
  "N23" [label="(24,0,0) PhoP-DNA interactions facilitate compact arrangement of PhoP_RECs and PhoP_DBDs, restricting 4-bp spacer between binding sites."];
  "N4" [label="(4,0,0) In promoters with suboptimal elements, transcription activators recruit RNAP or bend DNA to compensate for weaker σ-DNA interactions."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) Activator-containing RPo is termed transcription activation complex (TAC)."];
  "N6" [label="(6,0,0) Transcription activators from σ70 family are classified into four types based on binding site positioning relative to -35 element."];
  "N8" [label="(8,0,0) The complex formed by transcription repressors is termed transcription repression complex (TRC)."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper-level aim is to investigate the structural and mechanistic basis of Mycobacterium tuberculosis PhoP dual transcription regulation by studying how PhoP promoter binding, oligomeric promoter architectures, and GlnR-linked repression contexts support activation and repression.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a bacterial response regulator has unclear dual activator/repressor roles, structural and mechanistic analysis of its promoter complexes and repression contexts can motivate a coherent research program."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Structural studies spanning PhoP activation-related promoter binding, promoter-architecture-dependent oligomeric states, and the GlnR-linked repression context can test how PhoP implements dual transcription regulation."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) PhoP repression of the nitrogen-metabolism gene amtB through cross-talk with GlnR identifies a PhoP-linked transcription-repression context that should be mechanistically examined, without assuming a specific competitive-binding or DNA-distortion mechanism."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) PhoP belongs to a regulatory context in which OmpR/PhoB-family response regulators can act as dual functional transcription regulators, motivating study of how PhoP supports activator and repressor roles."];
